\chapter{Taskgroups, fibers, and affine handles}
\label{ch:41}
\index{Taskgroups, fibers, and affine handles}

On the Linux/glibc direct-C11 profile, a \OnxCodeTerm{taskgroup} lexically owns its children. \OnxCodeTerm{launch \{ ... \}} schedules a stackful internal fiber with an admitted i64 result. \OnxCodeTerm{join} consumes the corresponding handle and returns that result. The scheduler multiplexes fibers on one native thread. Each reached handle has to be joined or consumed by cancellation on all normal paths. Detach is outside the profile.

\section{Reason about completion}
For each launch, identify the owner group, the child result, and the parent path that consumes the handle. A conditional with a join on only one branch is useful as a rejection fixture. In a positive case, force one child to wait for another through a channel and assert the result after join. Distinguish a build failure that rejects an affine handle leak from a runtime deadlock between well-typed waits. Stackful fiber execution retains call stacks. Verified async/await frame lowering remains a separate missing capability.

